## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design for real-time monocular 6-DoF pose with few-shot adaptation on edge hardware, including data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Lightweight backbone plus specialized heads for detection, segmentation, and pose
- On-device few-shot adaptation via adapters or compact fine-tuning
- Synthetic+real data and domain randomization
- Occlusion robustness and reprojection-based losses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: simple pose reprojection loss sketch
function poseLoss(pred, gt, intr) {
  // pred: 6-DoF [rx,ry,rz,tx,ty,tz], gt: ground truth
  // project 3D corners to 2D and compare
  const pts3D = corners3D();
  const pts2D = project(pts3D, pred, intr);
  return meanSquaredError(pts2D, gt2D);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure adaptation effectiveness on unseen feature types with minimal labeling?
- What strategies mitigate drift when features are tiny or heavily occluded?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design production-ready, on-device face verification systems with robust anti-spoofing capabilities under strict computational constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge-optimized inference architectures
- 3D head pose estimation from facial landmarks
- Temporal liveness detection for spoof resistance
- Parameter-efficient 5-shot adaptation via adapter layers
- Hybrid synthetic-to-real data generation strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: unified inference pipeline
function processFrame(frame) {
  const backbone = extractFeatures(frame);
  const detection = faceDetectionHead(backbone);
  const liveness = temporalLivenessHead(backbone);
  return { detection, liveness };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a real-time monocular system integrating segmentation, 3D pose estimation with scene priors, grasp-point planning, and physics-based stability reasoning—plus efficient edge adaptation with minimal shots.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation using environmental priors
- Grasp-point prediction in cluttered scenes
- Differentiable physics modules for stability evaluation
- On-device few-shot adaptation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for the model heads
function forward(img, shelfPrior) {
  const features = backbone(img);
  const segmentation = segmentationHead(features);
  const pose = poseHead(features, shelfPrior);
  const grasp = graspHead(features, pose);
  const stability = physicsPrior(grasp, pose);
  return { segmentation, pose, grasp, stability };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests system-level thinking for 3D reconstruction under dynamic scenes and edge-runtime constraints, plus evaluation rigor.

## Key Concepts

- Dense 3D representation (TSDF) with dynamic masking
- Lightweight pose estimation and masked fusion
- Dynamic voxel confidence and decay for occlusions
- Edge latency targets and robust evaluation metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: update TSDF with dynamic masking
function updateVoxelTSDF(voxel, depth, pose, isDynamic) {
  if (isDynamic) return; // skip static updates for dynamic voxels until confirmed
  const sdf = projectDepthToVoxel(depth, pose, voxel.coord);
  voxel.tsdf = (voxel.tsdf * voxel.weight + sdf) / (voxel.weight + 1);
  voxel.weight += 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you integrate semantic priors to improve dynamic masking in cluttered warehouses?
- What ablation model would you run to measure the impact of dynamic masking on map quality and drift over 5 seconds?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical monocular 6-DoF pose estimator that handles symmetry, occlusion, and online adaptation in a real-world manufacturing setting. Emphasizes differentiable refinement, data strategies, and latency planning.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for pose refinement
- Symmetry handling and occlusion robustness
- Synthetic data + domain randomization
- Online adaptation with few-shot updates
- Real-time latency targets (embedded GPU)

## Code Example
```javascript
class PoseHead {
  constructor(featDim) {
    this.fcRot = linear(featDim, 4); // quaternion
    this.fcTrans = linear(featDim, 3);
  }
  forward(feat) {
    const q = normalize(this.fcRot(feat), 2);
    const t = this.fcTrans(feat);
    return { quat: q, trans: t };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate symmetry handling for objects with known symmetry? 
- How would you mitigate catastrophic forgetting during online adaptation?
